WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.840
Yeah so this is a really
interesting puzzle

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:06.000
because it turns out the puzzle itself

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.000
is wrong. So not only do you have to

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.599
solve the puzzle you have to figure out

00:00:09.599 --> 00:00:11.200
what the puzzle really is.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.280
It combines two of my interests: error

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:16.400
correction and sequences.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:18.320
So I found this sequence on a website,

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.000
actually a Dutch website,

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:22.080
although that's irrelevant. It's a

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.720
sequence that came up as part of a quiz.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:27.279
The quiz begins by asking you to

00:00:27.279 --> 00:00:28.240
identify

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:32.630
various things, looks like 
some kind of a thing-

00:00:32.630 --> 00:00:34.640
(Brady: I know that word!
I know that word;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:35.760
voorwerp,

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.520
that means object.
- Aha! The third page

00:00:39.520 --> 00:00:43.120
has a number sequence
on it and they ask you

00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:45.440
what's the number that goes where the

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.559
question mark is?

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:49.600
And the sequence goes 1,

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:52.800
2, 6, 21,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:58.079
85, question mark, followed by 2586. Now

00:00:58.079 --> 00:00:59.920
you stare at this

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.719
and you cannot figure it out; I didn't

00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:03.840
see any pattern.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.080
So at the end you can look up the answer

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.600
and the answer they give is 430.

00:01:13.600 --> 00:01:14.960
(So you had to cheat to look up the

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.840
answer?)
- I cheated to look up the answer,

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.520
because I couldn't figure it out! And

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.080
since I am supposed to be an expert in

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:23.600
sequences I wanted to know,

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.280
what is the sequence that I couldn't

00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:26.640
figure out? And once

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.799
I knew what they thought the answer was

00:01:28.799 --> 00:01:30.240
I could figure it out.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.920
And so what they did

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:35.920
had a mistake in it. How do you go from

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:37.360
6 to 21?

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.640
You add 1 and multiply by

00:01:40.640 --> 00:01:43.280
3 and we get 21. So how do

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:44.720
you go from 2 to 6?

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.159
You add 1 and you multiply by 2;

00:01:48.159 --> 00:01:52.240
add 1 you get 3 times 2 is 6.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:54.399
How do you go from 1 to 2? You add

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:57.119
1 and multiply by 1;

00:01:57.119 --> 00:01:59.280
1 plus 1 is 2 times 1 and you

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:00.640
get 2. Okay,

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:03.360
so so you stare at this and you say 'how

00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:05.600
do you go from 21 to 85?'

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.399
Well if you add 1 and multiply by 4

00:02:08.399 --> 00:02:10.800
you do not get 85, you get 88.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.040
The person making up the test did it in

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:14.239
the wrong order.

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:17.680
They multiplied by 4, to get 84,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.879
and they added 1 and they got 85.

00:02:20.879 --> 00:02:24.160
How did they go from 85 to 430?

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:27.440
They added 1

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:31.680
to get 86 and multiplied by 5

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:34.080
and got 430. 
- (So they started doing it)

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:35.840
(right again?)
- They started doing it right

00:02:35.840 --> 00:02:36.640
again,

00:02:36.640 --> 00:02:38.640
so that's the answer.
- (How long did it)

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.400
(take you to figure out what the mistake)

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:41.360
(they'd made was?)

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:43.680
Well the pattern- once you look at how

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:44.640
you might go -

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:45.840
this is something you always do when

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.280
you're looking at a sequence and there's

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:48.000
no obvious

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:49.920
mathematical rule - you say to yourself,

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:51.200
how can I go

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:54.239
from 2 to 6? How do I go from 6

00:02:54.239 --> 00:02:56.879
to 21? You try to think of a way

00:02:56.879 --> 00:02:58.800
to build it up and, well, there's a

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.319
this part is one of many possibilities

00:03:02.319 --> 00:03:05.760
but it works and so, well, all right then

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:07.949
it became clear that there was a mistake.

00:03:09.599 --> 00:03:11.120
[Preview] It's one of the
most basic things that

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.200
we can do. And even now,

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:15.680
even when we have digital images from

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:17.040
great big telescopes,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:18.959
we still get a lot of information by

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:22.640
looking at them with our eye.

